WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.429
The EAS  reaction can also be used to Introduce a sulphonic acid group

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:17.569
onto an aromatic ring. In this lesson, we're going to look at two sets of conditions that can cause this reaction to occur.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:26.149
We will first look at the sulfonation of benzene with sulfuric acid to produce our sulfonic acid.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.329
This will shed light on the subtleties of our second mechanism, where we use sulfur trioxide.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:40.040
To produce the electrophile needed for this reaction two molecules of sulfuric acid must react.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:45.529
This equilibrium is going on to a small extent in a solution of sulfuric acid.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.180
Let's add on our  lone pairs to keep track of them a little bit better and

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.160
then we can show the OH group of one molecule of sulfuric acid

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.580
becoming pronated by the acidic proton on a second molecule of sulfuric acid.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.889
This gives the conjugate base of sulfuric acid and this oxonium

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:14.749
Keeping track of our charges, notice that our first molecule gave up a proton and gained an extra lone pair.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:17.539
This gives it the formal negative charge on oxygen.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:24.349
The second molecule of sulfuric acid shared a lone pair to become protonated and

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:28.279
that's how we get the oxonium ion with the positive charge.

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:33.379
Now we're going to show water act as a leaving group to generate our

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:36.589
electrophile, but notice two atoms away

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:44.629
we have another oxygen atom so we can facilitate the departure of the leaving group by using its lone pair.

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:49.608
From this process we get water and a protonated form of sulfur trioxide,

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:56.269
which is a great electrophile. Benzene can now attack with its double bond to give an

00:01:56.609 --> 00:02:03.498
intermediate carbocation. The resonance stabilized conjugate base of sulfuric acid is not a good nucleophile

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:09.559
so we can show water deprotonating this molecule to form our sulfonic acid.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:18.110
The reaction we just shown is rather slow. This is because we're depending on the equilibration of a strong acid in solution

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.130
to form small amounts of protonated sulfur trioxide,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.839
which can then push the reaction forward when it reacts with benzene,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:32.480
but that overall equilibration is not very favorable so this reaction does take some time.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:39.769
We can carry out this reaction more efficiently using sulfur trioxide in sulfuric acid

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.700
This reagent is called fuming sulfuric acid and when you open the bottle it does indeed smoke.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.640
Do this in a fume hood; it's very corrosive.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.780
This mechanism can be drawn a couple ways. Notice above we generated a protonated form of sulfur trioxide

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:03.289
Now the protonation of SO3 may be the first step in this reaction under certain conditions;

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.649
however, benzene can also attack sulfur trioxide

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:15.290
directly, and we'll show that mechanism here. Benzene attacks SO3 pushing the electrons up onto oxygen

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:24.290
restoring aromaticity in this molecule is a strong driving force. So some people opt to show a spontaneous deprotonation here.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:32.119
But we also have appropriate bases in solution to do this deprotonation. If we use sulfur trioxide

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:38.839
to do the deprotonation, we can create that great electrophile that we generated in the above mechanism.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.319
So we'll show another molecule of sulfur trioxide

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.760
deprotonating benzene and a final protonation of this anion will give our sulfonic acid,

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:52.489
which was drawn above.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:04:00.799
Let's take a look at the properties of the sulfonic acid derivative that we've just made. This is an electron withdrawing group.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:06.020
We can show how electron density is pulled out of the ring using one of the sulfur–

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:12.980
oxygen double bonds. This resonance form has positive character at the ortho position of the aromatic ring.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:20.779
We can continue to push electrons around the ring and show that there's positive character at the para and the other

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.330
ortho position of this aromatic ring. Thus, the sulfonic acid group is a

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:34.940
deactivating group. It pulls the electrons out of the ring that benzene needs to undergo further electrophilic aromatic substitution

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.519
reactions. The electrons within the ring are less available to attack

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:45.559
electrophiles. On this compound the meta carbons are the most electron rich,

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.869
so when we treat benzenesulfonic acid with a powerful enough electrophile—

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.249
remember the electrophile needs to be pretty powerful now that the ring is deactivated—

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.170
the new group will be introduced at the meta position. In this lesson, we looked at two methods for the

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.570
sulfonation of benzene.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.339
We then looked at the properties of the derivative that we made: benzenesulfonic acid.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:17.450
We saw that the introduction of this group makes benzene less reactive to further EAS reactions;

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.770
however, when it does react the groups will be added at the meta position.

